# Core: type checking, pattern compilation, FBIP (adamas-core).
#
# A literal brace is written in Fluent as {"{"} and {"}"}.

core-not-a-type = expected a type, found `{ $term }` of type `{ $ty }`
core-not-a-function = expected a function, found a value of type `{ $ty }`
core-mismatch = type mismatch: expected `{ $expected }`, found `{ $found }`
core-lambda-multiplicity = the lambda has multiplicity { $found }, but the type requires { $expected }
core-usage-violation = `{ $name }` is declared with multiplicity { $declared }, but used { $actual }
core-cannot-infer = the type of `{ $term }` cannot be inferred: an annotation is needed
core-unknown-constant = definition `{ $name }` not found
core-level-arity = `{ $name }` takes { $expected } level parameters, { $found } given
core-erased-constant = `{ $name }` is declared with multiplicity 0 and is not available at runtime
core-partial-constant = `{ $name }` is not total and cannot appear in a type or a proof
core-duplicate-definition = definition `{ $name }` already exists
core-linear-definition = definition `{ $name }` cannot be linear: there is no whole-program accounting
core-unsettled-level = levels `{ $left }` and `{ $right }` do not reduce: the solution is ambiguous, an annotation is needed
core-unsettled-term = postponed constraint failed: `{ $left }` and `{ $right }`
core-not-a-data-sort = `{ $name }` is declared as an inductive type, but ends in `{ $found }`
core-data-parameters = `{ $name }` is declared with { $expected } parameters, but has only { $found } binders
core-not-a-data-type = `{ $name }` is not an inductive type
core-constructor-parameter = constructor `{ $name }` must begin with the parameters of `{ $data }`, but parameter #{ $index } differs
core-constructor-result = constructor `{ $name }` must return `{ $data }`, but returns `{ $found }`
core-not-an-effect-sort = `{ $name }` is declared as an effect, but ends in `{ $found }`
core-operation-parameter = operation `{ $name }` must repeat the parameters of `{ $effect }`, but differs at parameter { $index }
core-not-strictly-positive = constructor `{ $name }` uses `{ $data }` in a negative position
core-non-uniform-parameter = constructor `{ $name }` applies `{ $data }` to something other than its parameters
core-constructor-universe = a field of constructor `{ $name }` lives in `Type { $field }`, but the type lives in `Type { $sort }`
core-duplicate-field = field `{ $name }` is declared twice
core-open-dependent-record = field `{ $name }` depends on an earlier one, and such a record cannot be open
core-record-fields = { $found } record fields where the type has { $expected }
core-not-a-row = the record tail is not a row: `{ $ty }`
core-not-a-record = projection from a non-record: `{ $ty }`
core-closed-with = `{ $ty }` is closed: it is updated by rebuilding, not by overriding
core-no-such-field = `{ $ty }` has no field `{ $name }`
core-erased-field = field `{ $name }` is erased: it has no value
core-not-a-data-value = matching on `{ $data }`, but the value has type `{ $ty }`
core-erased-scrutinee = the match on `{ $data }` is declared with multiplicity 0, but the branch is chosen by the value
core-split-shape = record match: { $why }
core-case-parameters = the match on `{ $data }` declares { $found } parameters, but the type has { $expected }
core-non-exhaustive = the match on `{ $data }` does not cover constructor `{ $constructor }`
core-redundant-branch = redundant branch `{ $constructor }` in the match on `{ $data }`
core-branch-order = branches of `{ $data }` must follow the declaration order: expected `{ $expected }`, found `{ $found }`
core-level-var-out-of-scope = `{ $name }` uses level parameter u{ $var } with arity { $arity }
core-hole-misfit = an inferred argument does not fit its type `{ $hole }`: its place requires `{ $wanted }`
core-mult-entangled = the multiplicity parameters of `{ $name }` are tied together: the body is not checked under every combination - write the product in the multiplicity position, `(q * r x : a)`
pattern-clause-arity = clause #{ $clause }: { $found } patterns for { $expected } arguments
pattern-unbound-in-body = clause #{ $clause }: the body refers beyond its own variables
pattern-ill-typed-type = the type of the definition is not a type: { $error }
pattern-not-matchable = nothing to match: the value has type `{ $ty }`
pattern-impossible-pattern = clause #{ $clause }: `{ $constructor }` is impossible here - the index requires `{ $expected }`, but the constructor gives `{ $found }`
pattern-stuck-index = constructor `{ $constructor }`: index `{ $found }` does not reduce to `{ $expected }`, there is nothing to unify
pattern-foreign-constructor = constructor `{ $constructor }` does not belong to type `{ $data }`
pattern-constructor-arity = constructor `{ $constructor }`: { $found } subpatterns for { $expected } fields
pattern-record-arity = tuple: { $found } components for { $expected } record fields
pattern-record-column = a tuple and a constructor or literal match the same value of type `{ $ty }`
pattern-non-exhaustive = not covered: `{ $example }`
pattern-unreachable-clause = clause #{ $clause } is unreachable: earlier clauses cover it
pattern-literal-pattern = literal `{ $written }` does not match a value of type `{ $ty }`: { $why }
pattern-verdict = a literal pattern answers `Bool` (§4.3), but { $why }
fbip-shape = `{ $matched }` is matched ({ $matched_fields } fields), but `{ $built }` is built ({ $fields } fields): reuse rewrites the slots of the matched cell, and the shapes must agree
fbip-alive = the matched `{ $matched }` is used again, so it stays alive: there is nothing to rewrite, and `{ $built }` ({ $fields } fields) is allocated afresh
fbip-taken = the matched `{ $matched }` is already rewritten: `{ $built }` ({ $fields } fields) is built second and has nothing to rewrite - the branch allocates
core-unbound-index = variable #{ $index } out of context
core-ambiguous-level = level ?{ $meta } is undetermined: add an annotation
core-unsolved-definition-row = an unsolved row tail ?{ $meta } remains in definition `{ $name }`
core-unsolved-definition-level = an unsolved level ?{ $meta } remains in definition `{ $name }`
core-operation-row = operation `{ $name }` must perform exactly `{ $effect }` with its own parameters, but performs `{ $found }`
core-undischarged = effects `{ $wanted }` are not handled: the surrounding context allows `{ $ambient }`
core-ambiguous-term-owned = the implicit argument of `{ $owner }` is not inferred: the use site does not determine it - give the result a type (with a `let` annotation or a signature)
core-ambiguous-term = argument ?{ $meta } is not inferred: write it explicitly
frame-callee = the function of an application
frame-argument = the argument of an application
frame-domain = the domain
frame-codomain = the codomain
frame-body = the lambda body
frame-binding-type = the type of a binding
frame-stated = the stated type
frame-binding-value = the value of a binding
frame-binding-body = the body of `let`
frame-scrutinee = the matched value
frame-motive = the motive of a match
frame-branch = branch #{ $index }
frame-member-type = the type of group member #{ $index }
frame-member-body = the body of group member #{ $index }
frame-constructor = constructor #{ $index }
alloc-through = calls `{ $callee }`, which{" "}
alloc-construct = builds a value with constructor `{ $name }` - that is the Perceus heap. There are three ways out (§5.1): a linear or unique input, where reuse is guaranteed; a region `{"{"}Alloc r{"}"}`; not constructing. The compiler has none of the first two yet, so the third remains
alloc-record = builds a record - that is the Perceus heap. There are three ways out (§5.1): a linear or unique input, where reuse is guaranteed; a region `{"{"}Alloc r{"}"}`; not constructing. The compiler has none of the first two yet, so the third remains
alloc-closure = creates a closure: the lambda stands beyond the parameters, and its environment goes to the heap
alloc-partial = applies `{ $name }` partially, and the missing arguments wait in a closure
alloc-operation = calls operation `{ $name }`: whether it allocates is decided by the branch of the handler that intercepts it, and that handler is unknown at the call site. The tail-resumptive verdict (§3.4) is computed at the `handle` site, and that site is not written here
alloc-resumption = installs handler `{ $effect }`, whose branch `{ $operation }` calls `resume` outside tail position: the continuation has to be captured into a heap segment (§3.4, §5.1). A tail branch leaves the continuation alone, an abortive one discards it - both are fine
alloc-multi-shot = installs `handleMulti` over `{ $effect }`: an ω-resumption does not promise "at most once", and the segment is copied to the heap on every resumption (§3.4, "The cost of multi-shot")
alloc-opaque = calls `{ $name }`, which has no body and no `@noalloc`: across the boundary the obligation is declared, not inferred from someone else's code (§5.1)
alloc-boxing = applies a function value: the argument travels in a closure slot, a slot holds a pointer, and a flat argument is boxed at this boundary (§4.11). The verdict has no types, so every such application counts as allocating
alloc-call = calls `{ $name }`, which allocates
why-literal-not-primitive = only a primitive is matched by a literal (§4.11)
why-literal-out-of-type = the literal does not fit this type (§4.3)
why-bool-undeclared = it is not declared
why-bool-not-family = it is declared, but not as a family
why-bool-two-constructors = it must have exactly two constructors - `True` and `False`
why-split-erased = the match is erased, but the fields are needed as values
why-split-open = the record is open, and only a closed one can be matched
why-split-fields = the fields of the match differ from the record's, or come in another order
why-split-parametric = a field with its own parameters cannot be matched
verdict-tail = tail-resumptive
verdict-abortive = abortive
verdict-general = general
trap-division-by-zero = division by zero
trap-lane-outside = lane number outside the vector width
trap-cell-outside = cell number outside the array length
trap-window-outside = vector window outside the array length
blocked-handler = effect `{ $label }` in a callback position: its handler is { $verdict }, so control will not return to the foreign frame normally (§5.3). Between entering the callback and returning from it lies a frame built by C: we cannot unwind it and have no right to skip it. The cancellation idiom is to record the intent and return normally, and act on our own side of the boundary
blocked-unhandled = effect `{ $label }` in a callback position is discharged by no handler of the program: an unhandled operation aborts execution, and control never returns to the foreign frame (§5.3)
blocked-open = the callback's row is open with tail `{ $tail }`: what comes beyond the written labels is unknown at the registration site, and the foreign frame requires an answer before the call (§5.3). Write the row closed
